# --- Secrets -------------------------------------------------------------
# Patterns, not literal filenames. This previously listed exactly
# "config.yaml" and "gs4creds.json", so a copy or rename (config_2026.yaml,
# creds.json) would have been committed silently. Note that "*.env" does NOT
# match a bare ".env" -- hence ".env*" below.
config*.yaml
config*.yml
!config.example.yaml
# `config*.yaml` matches on *basename*, so it catches root-level config.yaml and leaves
# config/injuries/<season>.yaml alone. That is the behaviour we want but it is incidental,
# so it is pinned: those files are hand-written severity judgements whose git history is the
# point, and a future tightening of the pattern above must not swallow them silently.
!config/injuries/
# Backups and editor droppings of the credential file. `config*.yaml` matches on
# basename and extension, so it does NOT catch config.yaml.bak / .orig / .save --
# which hold exactly the same secrets. Caught on 2026-08-24 when a `git add -A`
# staged config.yaml.bak carrying a live FantasyPros session and the ESPN cookies.
config.yaml.*
config*.yaml.*
*.yaml.bak
*.yaml.orig
*.yaml.save
*creds*.json
*credential*.json
service-account*.json
.env*
*.env
*.pem
*.key

# --- Python --------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
.venv/
venv/
env/
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# --- R -------------------------------------------------------------------
.Rproj.user
.Rhistory
.RData
.Ruserdata

# --- OS ------------------------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# --- Data ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Nothing under Data/ is tracked. S3 is the system of record -- see
# docs/plans/24-s3-data-flow.md -- and `python -m Scripts.sync --push` is how data
# reaches durability. Locally Data/ is a writer's scratch pad plus a read cache, and
# it is safe to delete: `--pull` rebuilds it.
#
# This used to be a long list of per-file exceptions, because the tiers differ in how
# expensive they are to rebuild and that mattered when the decision was per file. It
# still differs and it still matters, but the place to record it is the plan doc and
# the runbook, not here:
#
#   * Data/G2/ cannot be rebuilt at any price. FantasyPros serves no season
#     parameter, so a past board is gone the moment it stops being current, which is
#     why the archive exists at all (plan 18). It was the one tracked data directory
#     in the repo. It is now in S3 under archive/, exempt from version expiry, and
#     still reachable in git history at every commit up to ab477f9 -- untracking is
#     not deletion.
#   * Data/NFL/{routes,red_zone}.parquet need full play-by-play, so a cold backfill
#     is minutes rather than seconds. That is why the nflverse pulls are mirrored to
#     S3 at all rather than just regenerated.
#   * Data/Equivalence/ is deliberately NOT in S3 either. At 74 MB it is half the
#     tree, and it is before/after debug snapshots from the espn-api 0.46.0
#     migration -- evidence about a bug that is fixed, not live data.
#   * Data/refresh_status.json is machine state, not data. It says whether the
#     nightly job ran, which is a fact about this laptop rather than about football,
#     so it is neither tracked nor uploaded.
Data/

# Objects downloaded from S3, cached by ETag. Disposable by design.
Data/.s3cache/
